course: world
lesson: 33
title: Digestion: from mouth to intestines
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Food is too big and lumpy for the body to use, so it has to be broken down into tiny pieces small enough to pass into the blood. This is called digestion.
explain: It starts in the mouth: teeth chew the food and saliva wets it and begins to break down starch.
explain: When you swallow, the food goes down a tube called the oesophagus (food pipe) into the stomach, where strong acid and juices churn it into a thick soup.
explain: In the small intestine, a tube about six metres (about 20 feet) long folded up in your belly, the food is broken down fully and passes into the blood.
explain: What is left goes into the large intestine, where water is taken back into the body, and the waste leaves the body as faeces.
explain: Think of it like a factory line: each part does one job and passes the work on to the next.
fact: Digestion breaks food into pieces small enough to pass into the blood.
fact: Digestion starts in the mouth with chewing and saliva.
fact: Saliva starts to break down starch, as in bread and rice, into sugar.
fact: The oesophagus (food pipe) carries food from the mouth to the stomach.
fact: The stomach mixes food with acid and juices.
fact: Most digested food passes into the blood in the small intestine.
fact: The small intestine of an adult is about six metres (about 20 feet) long.
fact: The large intestine takes water back from what is left.
fact: Fibre from fruit, vegetables and whole grains helps food move through the intestines.
fact: Without enough fibre, food moves slowly and a person can get constipated, which means finding it hard to poo.
fact: The liver makes bile, which helps to digest fat.
fact: Food passes along the food pipe and intestines because muscles in their walls squeeze it along.
exercise: What is digestion? | Breaking food into pieces small enough to pass into the blood | Breathing air into the lungs and then sending it down to the stomach | Pumping blood around the body | Growing new bones
exercise: Where does digestion begin? | In the mouth | In the stomach | In the large intestine | In the liver
exercise: What does saliva begin to break down? | Starch | Bone | Water | Oxygen
exercise: Which tube carries food from the mouth to the stomach? | The oesophagus | The windpipe | An artery | The large intestine
exercise: What happens to food in the stomach? | It is churned with acid and juices | It passes straight into the lungs | It is turned into bone | Water is taken out of it and it turns hard
exercise: Where does most digested food pass into the blood? | The small intestine | The mouth | The oesophagus | The windpipe
exercise: About how long is the small intestine of an adult? | About six metres | About six centimetres | About sixty metres | About as long as a finger
exercise: What is the main job of the large intestine? | To take water back into the body | To chew food into tiny pieces again | To make saliva for the next meal you eat | To pump blood down to the legs and feet
exercise: Which foods give us fibre? | Fruit, vegetables and whole grains | Meat, eggs and cheese from the fridge | Water and juice | Butter and oil
exercise: What does bile from the liver help to digest? | Fat | Water | Salt | Oxygen
exercise: What moves food along the food pipe and intestines? | Muscles in their walls squeezing it | Gravity alone pulling it down all the way | Air from the lungs blowing it along inside | The heart pushing it along with each beat
exercise: Why do we chew food well? | Small pieces are easier to digest | Chewing makes food heavier | Chewing turns food into blood | Chewing cools food down for the lungs
exercise: In what order does food travel? | Mouth, food pipe, stomach, small intestine, large intestine | Mouth, stomach, food pipe, large intestine, small intestine | Mouth, lungs, stomach, small intestine, large intestine | Stomach, mouth, food pipe, small intestine, large intestine
exercise: What leaves the body at the end of digestion? | The waste that could not be used | All the food we ate, unchanged and whole | The oxygen from the food | The blood from the stomach
test: An astronaut eats in space where there is almost no up or down. Can the food still reach the stomach? | Yes, muscles in the food pipe squeeze it along | No, food needs gravity to fall down | Only if the astronaut stands up | Only if the food is liquid
test: Hold a piece of bread in your mouth for a long time and it starts to taste a little sweet. Why? | Saliva breaks starch into sugar | The bread goes stale | The tongue adds sugar to it | The teeth squeeze sugar out of it
test: Which is true? | Food passes into the blood mostly in the small intestine | Food passes into the blood mostly in the mouth | The large intestine is where we chew | The stomach is where most of the water is taken back into the body
test: Someone eats very little fruit, vegetables or whole grains. What problem may they get? | Food moves slowly and they get constipated | Their bones grow too long | Their lungs fill with water | They breathe too fast
test: If the large intestine did not work, what would the body lose? | A lot of water | All its oxygen | All its saliva | Its sense of taste
test: Which part of digestion happens before swallowing? | Chewing and wetting food with saliva | Churning the food with acid and juices | Taking back water from waste in the gut | Passing the food into the blood vessels
test: Why must food be broken into tiny pieces before the body can use it? | Only tiny pieces can pass into the blood | Big pieces are too heavy to swallow | Tiny pieces taste better | Big pieces make the heart stop
test: A doctor talks about the organ that makes bile. Which organ is it? | The liver | The lungs | The heart | The brain
test: so where does my sandwich actually go into my blood | Mostly through the walls of the small intestine | Straight from the mouth into the blood | Through the walls of the stomach only | Through the lungs when we breathe
test: how come food goes down even if u eat upside down lol | Muscles in the food pipe squeeze it along | Gravity still pulls it down to the stomach | Saliva makes it slide on its own | The stomach sucks it down like a straw
test: my bro says digestion starts in the stomach. true? | No, it starts in the mouth with chewing and saliva | Yes, nothing happens until the stomach | Yes, the mouth only tastes the food | No, it starts in the small intestine after the stomach
test: Sam reckons fibre is useless cos we can't digest it. right? | No, fibre helps food move through the intestines | Yes, fibre does nothing in the body | Yes, fibre only makes food heavier | No, fibre gives us most of our energy
exam: Identify the location at which digestion commences. | The mouth | The stomach | The small intestine | The large intestine
exam: Identify the organ in which the greater part of digested food passes into the blood. | The small intestine | The stomach | The large intestine | The oesophagus
exam: State the principal function of the large intestine. | The recovery of water from what remains | The production of bile | The digestion of starch | The mixing of food with acid
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the movement of food through the alimentary canal (the food pipe, stomach and intestines). | It is propelled by the squeezing of muscles in the walls | It descends by the force of gravity alone, like a stone in a well | It is carried by a current of air | It is drawn along by the heart
